    {
      "id": "https://theconversation.com/the-world-is-experiencing-a-string-of-abnormally-hot-days-and-records-are-being-shattered-297633",
      "url": "https://theconversation.com/the-world-is-experiencing-a-string-of-abnormally-hot-days-and-records-are-being-shattered-297633",
      "title": "Global temperatures exceed pre-industrial average by 2°C for a week as El Niño intensifies",
      "content_html": "Preliminary data from Copernicus, the European Union’s climate-monitoring service, show global temperatures stayed more than 2°C above the 1850–1900 average for seven consecutive days, reaching 2.25°C above that baseline on Monday. These daily readings do not establish that the Paris Agreement’s temperature limit has been breached: that target is assessed over at least 20 years. Scientists attribute the spike to long-term human-caused warming combined with a strong El Niño.\n\nThe record run coincided with COP31 climate negotiations in Turkey, where nearly 200 countries failed to agree on language about moving away from fossil fuels. The UK Met Office forecasts an unusually sharp rise in atmospheric carbon dioxide in 2027 as drought weakens natural carbon sinks; one estimate puts the year-to-year increase at about 3.5 parts per million. The current El Niño began in June and is expected to continue until at least March.",
